Commands go on an undo stack, and undone commands go on a redo stack. Put the printed lines in order, first to last.

```js
const doc = [];
const done = [];
const undone = [];
const add = (t) => ({ run: () => doc.push(t), undo: () => doc.pop(), name: t });
function exec(c) { c.run(); done.push(c); undone.length = 0; }
function undo() { const c = done.pop(); c.undo(); undone.push(c); console.log("undo " + c.name); }
function redo() { const c = undone.pop(); c.run(); done.push(c); console.log("redo " + c.name); }
exec(add("a")); exec(add("b")); exec(add("c"));
undo(); undo(); redo();
exec(add("d"));
undo();
console.log(doc.join(""));
```

An editor saves mementos onto a stack and restores from them. Put the printed lines in order, first to last.

```csharp
var editor = new Editor();
var history = new Stack<Editor.Memento>();
editor.Type("a"); history.Push(editor.Save());
editor.Type("b"); history.Push(editor.Save());
editor.Type("c");
Console.WriteLine(editor.Text);
editor.Restore(history.Pop()); Console.WriteLine(editor.Text);
editor.Type("x"); Console.WriteLine(editor.Text);
editor.Restore(history.Pop()); Console.WriteLine(editor.Text);
editor.Restore(new Editor().Save()); Console.WriteLine($"[{editor.Text}]");

class Editor {
    public string Text { get; private set; } = "";
    public void Type(string s) => Text += s;
    public Memento Save() => new(Text);
    public void Restore(Memento m) => Text = m.State;
    public record Memento(string State);
}
```

